# Level 21, reached by password BXBS, then a *spin* rather than a box.
#
# **What this is aimed at is a creature that chases.** `$81:9B6B` is on level
# 21's display list for most of `movies/level21.zmv` -- 96 of 126 sampled frames,
# three copies at once -- and every call it makes takes the ignore path, so five
# of its seven sites had read zero since it was ported. The reason is in the two
# position dumps side by side: these things walk *at* the player, and the box
# walk's legs are 180 frames long, so the player spends almost all of its time
# facing away from the crowd it is towing. A shot leaves in the direction you are
# facing, so a chaser is behind it by construction.
#
# The tail is the same twelve directions at 30 frames instead of 180
# (`tools/make_spin_probe.py`). Four legs of 30 cancel out, so the player stays
# roughly where it started and faces all four directions once a second, which is
# what puts a shot into something that is walking into you. That is the whole
# difference between 0 hits and 35 over the same 2,100 frames.
#
# It takes `d9b6b_hit` and `d9b6b_died` -- 35 each, and the fact that those two
# numbers are *equal* is itself the finding: every hit this creature takes kills
# it, so `d9b6b_survived` needs a weapon weaker than one damage and there is no
# such thing. It also takes `f534_ignore`, which nothing had.
#
# The spin is not universally better. Run on levels 5, 17 and 49 it added nothing
# at all -- a creature that does not come to you has to be walked to, and the
# spin trades ground for aim. `--leg 180 --lookback 30` is the compromise.
